There's a second flock already drawn, for a Stage of birds that has portraits and no
decks yet. That's the Goose Mother on the left.

## 44 cards to 121

A Stage of cat Villains needs cat decks, and the card pool was 44 cards of mostly
Alpha. So cards now have a track of their own, outside the milestones. A card starts
from its current Oracle data, and gets sorted into one of four piles. Ready means the
engine can already express it. Blocked means it needs a new primitive. Set Aside means
it needs something new on screen, which starts with a prototype. Unsuited means it
could never mean anything in this game. Ready cards go in, at most 20 a session, and
the rest wait in a file grouped by what they're waiting on.

The original rule still holds, that a mechanic only enters the game with the first
card that needs it. It just gets exercised a lot more often now. The pool is at 121
cards from 45 sets, and getting there added Equipment, +1/+1 counters, lifelink,
tokens, modal spells, flashback, the legend rule, library searches, cycling, ward,
Auras, X costs and surveil, each one built test-first for a specific card. Oblivion
Ring brought exile that undoes itself, and a way to draw it, with the exiled card
tucked behind the Ring showing only its name. Brimaz alone needed four new pieces,
since he makes a token that enters attacking when he attacks and one that enters
blocking when he blocks.

## Winning pays

Beating a Villain now opens a Spoils screen. You get gold, and one to three cards
drawn from the Villain's own deck, with better odds of more at higher Ranks. Every
nonland copy in the deck is one even ticket, so there's no rarity weighting. The decks
are the tuning, and a tougher Villain pays better because its deck is built richer.
You take everything. There's no pick-one-of-three and no skip, since won cards go to
your Collection and not your deck, and what you don't want can be sold once Stores
work. A loss or a draw pays nothing.

Mods got a real definition before anything hands them out. A Mod has a rarity, an
Augmentation, which is what it does to the card, and a Mod Cost, which is what it adds
to the card's mana cost. Its text is generated the way rules text is: "Modified card
gets +1/+1 and gains trample. It costs {1} more to cast." Names got rules too. A
card's Mods are named in alphabetical order so the name doesn't depend on the order
you applied them, and the same Mod twice becomes Very. A Plains with Ancient once and
Violent twice is an Ancient Very Violent Plains. Three times is Extremely.

## Sharper tools

The whole interface got a theme overhaul, and the main menu now has a pixel soldier
standing on its window, with a little wisp of light floating around him. The Info
Panel in an encounter was rebuilt after it turned into the place where every
yes-or-no question gets asked, like whether to pay a ward cost or how much to pay for
X.

The test runner got stricter. Godot had been reporting 687 leaked objects at exit, and
they turned out to be the test framework's own, kept alive by an engine fault that one
specific class of mine happened to trigger. That's fixed, and a test run now fails if
anything leaks at all. The suite also runs across several threads now.

## Where it stands

There are 121 cards, 69 decision records and around 3,790 test functions, up from
2,370 five days ago. Kitty Keep is the only Stage with art, Villains and decks of its
own. The other four colors are still placeholders.

Next is making a run its full length, five Stages of rising Tier in five different
colors, with proper screens for winning and losing. After that come a reveal screen
before each fight, Armor, and the Store and Satchel, so gold has somewhere to go.
Then the other four colors need their own Kitty Keeps.
